  "final_answer": "I haven't added the position. Two parts of the request don't match the market data, and I'd have had to guess to fill either one in.\n\n**1. The date.** Today is Sunday 2026-09-27, so \"Saturday\" is 2026-09-26. US exchanges were closed that day, and the price history has no bar for it. Entering 2026-09-26 would record a trade that couldn't have filled. If the order was placed Saturday, it won't fill until Monday 09-28. The fill could also have been Friday 09-25. I don't know which one is right.\n\n**2. The price.** $301 is far below where JPM has been trading:\n- Friday 09-25: low 338.64, high 343.31, close 343.06\n- The whole week of 09-21 to 09-25: low 335.28\n- JPM last traded near $301 in early June (06-02 to 06-04, roughly 294 to 311). It has been above about $323 since mid-June.\n\nSo $301 could be a typo (maybe 341, which is inside Friday's range), a limit order that hasn't filled, or an older purchase.\n\n**What's in the portfolio now (unchanged):** 40 JPM at an average cost of $244.60 ($9,784 total), purchase date 2026-02-10.\n\n**What the add would do, once confirmed:** add 15 shares, bringing the position to 55. The new average cost depends on the price:\n- At $301: $259.96\n- At $341, for example: $270.89\n\nI need a confirmed trade date and fill price from the user before calling `portfolio_add_position`. Adding shares averages them into the existing position, so a wrong price would be mixed into the cost basis.",
